/* The application's design tokens.

   ⚠ **The name is the convention and the whole of the configuration.** The build step globs
   `**/vixen.ui.vcss`, finds this, and every class name in every `.vxml` and every `.cs` in the
   project resolves against it. One per project — two would be two palettes, which is the thing a
   token model exists to prevent, so the build refuses rather than picking.

   ⚠ **A project with no theme file still has tokens.** Everything below layers over the palette the
   engine ships — Tailwind v4's own twenty-six ramps in oklch — so `bg-slate-800` works here without
   being declared. `--color-*: initial;` is how an application that wants none of them says so, and
   `--color-blue-500: initial;` removes exactly one.

   A token is a custom property, so declaring `--color-brand` does two things at once: it makes
   `var(--color-brand)` available to hand-written CSS, and it tells the generator that `bg-brand`,
   `text-brand` and `border-brand` exist. */

@theme {
    /* 4px, which makes `p-2` eight and `p-4` sixteen and everybody able to see that one is twice the
       other. It is also the engine's default, so this line is a statement rather than a change — and
       it is the one to edit when the whole interface should breathe more. */
    --spacing: 4px;

    /* The one colour this application calls its own. */
    --color-brand: oklch(74% 0.13 195);
}
